00:41:41,960 --> 00:41:48,960
Acts chapter 16, verse 25.

349
00:41:48,960 --> 00:41:53,960
Around midnight, Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns. So Paul and Silas are in jail.

350
00:41:53,960 --> 00:41:58,960
They're singing hymns to God, and the other prisoners were listening.

351
00:41:58,960 --> 00:42:02,960
Suddenly, there was a massive earthquake, and the prison was shaking to its foundations.

352
00:42:02,960 --> 00:42:08,960
All the doors immediately flew open, and the chains of every prisoner fell off.

353
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:16,960
The jailer woke up to see the prison doors wide open. He assumed the prisoners had escaped, so he drew his sword to kill himself.

354
00:42:16,960 --> 00:42:22,960
But Paul shouted to him, "Stop. Don't kill yourself. We are all here."

355
00:42:22,960 --> 00:42:39,960
I think I could make an argument that miraculously having the doors fling open and the chains fall off might have been a sign that God was meeting a need of Paul and Silas, and that need was to get out of jail.

356
00:42:39,960 --> 00:42:51,960
I think it would be fair enough to say that Paul and Silas needed to get out of jail, or at least desperately wanted to get out of jail, and it seems like God did a miraculous work to make that a possibility for them.

357
00:42:51,960 --> 00:43:07,960
If I was them, I wouldn't have looked left or looked right. I would have just bolted out of that cell. But they were available. They were aware of their surroundings. They saw a man who was in desperate need because of what God had done.

358
00:43:07,960 --> 00:43:20,960
It doesn't say the Holy Spirit told them, "Don't go out of jail." Every sign of the Spirit is pointing to God is letting them out of jail.

359
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:29,960
And yet something about their lives saw a person in need and said, "That need is going to trump my need right now. I'm going to be obedient to Jesus."

360
00:43:29,960 --> 00:43:34,960
Even if it means I have to stay back in this prison cell and look what happened because of their obedience.

361
00:43:34,960 --> 00:43:46,960
The jailer called for the lights and ran to the dungeon and fell down trembling before Paul and Silas. Then he brought them out and asked, "Sirs, what must I do to be saved?"

362
00:43:47,960 --> 00:43:51,960
Because of this encounter, an entire family comes into the kingdom of God.

363
00:43:51,960 --> 00:43:56,960
What if we made space for the needs of others around us?

364
00:43:56,960 --> 00:44:05,960
What if we were people who made room to have eyes to see and hands to help those in need?

365
00:44:05,960 --> 00:44:09,960
How might God use our bold obedience to expand his kingdom on earth as it is in heaven?

366
00:44:09,960 --> 00:44:16,960
So, as we've established at the beginning of our time, most of us who are followers of Jesus know we are called to proclaim,

367
00:44:16,960 --> 00:44:21,960
Jesus to the world around us, but we often opt out of this responsibility.

368
00:44:21,960 --> 00:44:25,960
I think this is because many of us don't have a clear picture of what this should look like.

369
00:44:25,960 --> 00:44:28,960
We put too much pressure on ourselves to win converts.

370
00:44:28,960 --> 00:44:31,960
We're afraid of how we will be perceived. We don't feel equipped.

371
00:44:31,960 --> 00:44:35,960
My question is, what if we've made it too complicated?

372
00:44:35,960 --> 00:44:43,960
What if we stopped making evangelism a box that we check, a religious duty that we go out and do?

373
00:44:44,960 --> 00:44:49,960
What if we stopped viewing it as recruitment into a heavenly pyramid scheme, which nobody wants to be a part of?

374
00:44:49,960 --> 00:44:56,960
And what if we just started making room in our lives to partner with what God is already doing in the lives of people around us?

375
00:44:56,960 --> 00:45:02,960
What if we cultivated God's kingdom on earth by proclaiming him clearly and obeying him boldly?

376
00:45:02,960 --> 00:45:08,960
What if we weren't worried about the results and just enjoyed the process of becoming a person of God's kingdom?

377
00:45:11,960 --> 00:45:20,960
As we come to a close, there's a phrase that Luke, the author of the Gospel of Luke, uses to describe Jesus.

378
00:45:20,960 --> 00:45:25,960
He uses it twice. And I want to bring this up as we transition to communion.

379
00:45:25,960 --> 00:45:30,960
We're going to take communion here at the end of service, because I think it connects these two ideas.

380
00:45:30,960 --> 00:45:37,960
Luke, in his Gospel, uses the phrase "the Son of Man" twice.

381
00:45:38,960 --> 00:45:43,960
He gives an explanation of what Jesus came to do, what was his purpose, what was his mission.

382
00:45:43,960 --> 00:45:48,960
He says, "The Son of Man came to seek and save the lost."

383
00:45:48,960 --> 00:45:52,960
What Jesus was all about, he came to seek and save the lost.

384
00:45:52,960 --> 00:45:55,960
People without hope, people who were like sheep without a shepherd.

385
00:45:55,960 --> 00:46:01,960
Lost without hope, without purpose, without identity, just trying to survive.

386
00:46:01,960 --> 00:46:05,960
How many of us know people all around us that are lost without hope, just trying to survive?

387
00:46:05,960 --> 00:46:10,960
Jesus saw them, he had compassion on them, he came to seek them and save them.

388
00:46:10,960 --> 00:46:20,960
How did Jesus go about this? The second time Luke uses the phrase, "The Son of Man," it says, "The Son of Man came eating and drinking."

389
00:46:20,960 --> 00:46:31,960
Jesus was so hospitable, he was so about making room and inviting people to the table that weren't invited to other tables,

390
00:46:31,960 --> 00:46:36,960
that the religious leaders and Pharisees saw him and they say, "You're a glutton and a drunkard."

391
00:46:36,960 --> 00:46:41,960
This was how Jesus went about seeking and saving the lost.

392
00:46:41,960 --> 00:46:45,960
He invited people who did not have a table to go to, to come to.

393
00:46:45,960 --> 00:46:50,960
Not his table, because he also didn't have a table, he usually just hijacked other people's tables.

394
00:46:50,960 --> 00:46:59,960
Jesus came to seek and save the lost, he did so.

395
00:47:00,960 --> 00:47:13,960
Christ's radical hospitality, making space, inviting others around the table into a conversation about what life could look like if you lived towards the kingdom of God, rather than the kingdoms of this world.

396
00:47:13,960 --> 00:47:26,960
Much of Christ's kingdom work was making room at a table for the people the world had rejected, had looked over.

397
00:47:27,960 --> 00:47:42,960
Christ's radical hospitality looked foolish to the world, didn't make any sense to the Romans why Jesus would have poor, powerless, persecuted, sick, prostitutes, tax collectors around his table.

398
00:47:42,960 --> 00:47:51,960
It made no cultural sense. There was no objective that he could have been trying to accomplish that would have brought him any power or prestige, which is what they were all about.

399
00:47:51,960 --> 00:47:56,960
Foolish to the world. And it was sinful to the religious. The people who thought they had it all together said,

400
00:47:56,960 --> 00:48:01,960
"If he really was a prophet of God, he'd never sit at those tables."

401
00:48:01,960 --> 00:48:08,960
But his radical hospitality changed the trajectory of human history, and the early church picked up on it.

402
00:48:08,960 --> 00:48:15,960
The early church's success, in my opinion, was mostly based on them being radically hospitable.

403
00:48:15,960 --> 00:48:22,960
The people of every language, nation, tribe, and tongue, every gender, every religious system, you're welcome at this table.

404
00:48:22,960 --> 00:48:28,960
Let's talk about the bread that we are breaking, and the wine that we are drinking.

405
00:48:28,960 --> 00:48:32,960
You're welcome to our table, but let us tell you why you're welcome at our table.

406
00:48:32,960 --> 00:48:38,960
You're welcome at our table because the king of the universe came, and his body was broken, and his blood was shed.

407
00:48:38,960 --> 00:48:43,960
And three days later, he rose from the dead, proving that he wasn't just a prophet or an itinerant preacher,

408
00:48:43,960 --> 00:48:49,960
that he was the creator of the universe. And he wants you at his table.

409
00:48:49,960 --> 00:48:50,960
Thank you.
